ion would come to the United States  so when they announced that they were we  were all very excited of course but none  of us ever thought that we would receive  the highest accolade that mission could  give which is the three stars if you  receive three Michelin stars today it's  a great thing  but the burden of responsibility has now  become that much heavier going forward  and every year we think about that every  day we think about that making sure that  we are responsible to those stars  Keller's expectations are in plain sight  in the French Laundry state-of-the-art  kitchen balance a sense of urgency with  thinness I'm here with you in the  kitchen in California and we're watching  your team at stay in New York  hi guys it all begins with respect for  the ingredients and the French laundries  ingredients couldn't be fresher many of  them grow across the street this is  celtuce our Chinese lettuce and it has a  nutty flavor almost like a artichoke and  so you eat the base of that as well as  the leaves as well as the leaves  Erin Kiefer is the head gardener  responsible for thousands of pounds of  produce weekly he brings the goods to  executive sous-chef Elliot Bell the  celtuce were doing a salad so we're  doing that with some Satsuma mandarins  and  this kitchen has been called one of the  greatest cooking spaces in America it  recently underwent a 10 million dollar  renovation it's something Keller never  could have imagined when he bought the  restaurant in 1994 I was on my last legs  I had just had a dismal time in Los  Angeles I'd lost my restaurant in New  York this was it for me and to see  what's happened here in this restaurant  and how its influenced so many other  things in our community and in our state  and in our country is something that is  I'm extremely proud and blessed I mean  you could not have a more charming  setting than this town  Keller took a walk with us down  Washington Street home to five of his  restaurants including who's Sean this  feels like I'm in Paris right now once I  walk in that's where we're trying to  achieve and his newest venture to the  surprise of many a casual Mexican eatery  and again we're in a completely  different vibe right helmed by Chef  Kalin Ulrich true so Kalin JB what does  it mean to you as a 27 year old chef to  have the stamp of approval and  partnership of Thomas Keller I'm very  humbled I'm very grateful for it he's  giving me the keys and he's telling me  grab the steering wheel  here's the menu do what's authentic do  what you want to do here you'll find  elevated street food like these tacos al  pastor fresh made of the all of the  Urraco from the coast of oaxaca speak  you eat in Mexico I mean I'm gonna say  that's the best taco ever ate in my life  I'm not gonna eat a better taco today  I'm done or Pescados around the idol  verde branzino cooked on a woodfired  grill and brushed with adobo Cheers  cheers to you  hmm absolutely spectacular Thomas  Keller's star power has drawn  high-profile chefs to Yountville like  Adam Perry Lang Thomas Keller really  paved the way for so many us chefs not  only graduating his kitchen but people  that have been marred and I'm one of  those who admired Thomas first so so  long and just to have the opportunity to  be here and give my form of expression  in my form of cooking through steak and  creating a neighborhood Steakhouse  that's not only just for tourists but  for locals as well it was a big deal for  me and that's really why I went for it  the so-called master of meat brought his  signature dish to Napa from his  Hollywood Steakhouse APL and you're  known for the tomahawk steak tell ya  about that yeah that's it this that's  this right over here this is aged for  about a hundred days yeah it's a  specialty of just one of my favorite  things to cook that will be gone I mean  the bark on that yeah thank you I know  the terminology but it's juicy and  tender with its magic when you can get  both of those appreciate that for fine  dining there's no place quite like  Yountville a gourmet destination  inspired by Thomas Keller the chef who  dared to take a risk  humbled and broken 25 years ago where do  you see yourself 25 years from now  hopefully on a golf course yeah  restaurants are part of my life they've  always been part of my life they think  they'll always continue to be part of my  life in one way or another no longer can  I do what the young chef the parties do  in the restaurant alarm could I do with  the sous chefs do or even the chef de  cuisine but I'm proud to say that  they're better than I am  and that's what continues to motivate me  is that every generation is gonna be  better than the generation before  because that generation gave them the  opportunities gave them the tools gave  them the training so I'm confident that  even if I'm on a golf course  that what's happening here the French  shocking will continue to represent the  level of standards that we have  established in this restaurant  25 years ago on a golf course of the  tomahawk we want a tomahawk right I know  but obviously we left very inspired and  full it was a really really great trip  and you said the best Mexican food you  ever had I I think that Chef Kalin  Ulrich trilling is just really making  magic it's very authentic he comes from  a family of teachers and chefs in Oaxaca  he's a very special that's saying some  all of it and you know you talk about  Thomas Keller success but I never heard  the story of his failures before he  found out about that quite it's all  about that that's where it begins Thank  You Jaime waxen  you

